# tests/assertions/wordb_basic.rxt — module `assertions`, [M6.2] WAVE B:
# the two WORD-BOUNDARY assertions `\b` and `\B`.
#
# EVERY EXPECTATION IN THIS FILE WAS PRODUCED BY libpcre2 10.46 (through
# tests/fuzz/pcre2_oracle, options = 0), never written by hand. Blocks whose
# pattern contains `\Z` additionally carry `# pcre2-only` for wave A's
# recorded reason (python's `\Z` IS PCRE2's `\z`); every other block is also
# checked by python3 `re`, which agrees with PCRE2 about `\b`/`\B` on ASCII
# byte subjects — checked cell for cell by this lane, 0 divergences.
#
# WHAT MAKES THIS FILE DIFFERENT FROM absolute.rxt: the `ms`/`ns` cells.
#
# `\b` and `\B` are the module's first CONTEXT assertions — their truth at a
# position depends on the bytes on EITHER SIDE of it, not on the position's
# rank — and `<prefix>_search(s, n, startpos, caps)` searches `s[startpos, n)`
# while `s[startpos-1]` sits OUTSIDE that window and INSIDE the subject. So a
# `startpos > 0` cell is not "the same test one byte along": it is the only
# thing in this corpus that can see mechanism 4 (assertions_design.md §3.8) at
# all. MEASURED there: 5 of 10 `startpos > 0` cells differ between searching
# `s` from `startpos` and searching the SLICE `s[startpos:]` from 0, and they
# differ in BOTH directions, so no conservative reading is available.
#
# THE `\B` BLOCKS AT `startpos > 0` ARE NOT DECORATION. §3.8.3.1's reverse
# TERMINATION defect is invisible to a leading-`\b` population: `\b`'s blind
# assumption (no left context means non-word) coincides with its own truth
# condition, so a `\b`-only suite reports clean against an implementation that
# throws matches away. `\B` inverts it. The named cell is `\Bfoo` on "xfoo" at
# startpos 1, which is (1,4) and not "no match", and it is below.
#
# WHAT THIS FILE CANNOT SEE, stated so nobody mistakes it for coverage. It
# pins ANSWERS only. It cannot see that a `\b`-free pattern's emitted bytes
# did not move (tests/codegen/run_wordctx_identity.sh), that the accept table
# is never indexed at `pos == n` or that no `sfound` is recorded blind at the
# reverse boundary (tests/codegen/run_codegen_tests.sh's [M6.2-WORDB] block),
# or that the composed state budget's refusal boundary is where this wave says
# it is (tests/assertions/run_assertions_tests.sh).
#
# THIS FILE IS ONE OF FOUR SHARDS wordb.rxt was split into 2026-08-21 (the
# [M6.2.2] plan row "wordb.rxt shard-split + PROCS tiering") so
# tests/harness/run.sh's file-granularity parallelism (PROCS=N) does not
# serialize the corpus section's wall time behind one 4,608-case file. Every
# case moved verbatim; nothing was added, removed, or reworded. This shard
# carries the family-wide header above UNCHANGED (only this line and the
# filenames in it were updated) plus THE TEN §3.8 CELLS and BOTH ENDS AND
# THE INTERIOR — the plain, un-anchored, non-capturing `\b`/`\B` forms. The
# other three shards, each with its own short pointer back here for the
# oracle rule and the mechanism-4 background: wordb_empty_compose.rxt (the
# empty-match family plus composition with the module's other position
# assertions), wordb_engattempt.rxt (the `^`-routed computed-goto half),
# wordb_vm.rxt (the capturing-group-forced VM half). See
# tests/assertions/CLAUDE.md for the full shard list and rationale.

# ====================================================================
# THE TEN §3.8 CELLS, verbatim, plus their startpos sweeps.
# Five of the ten differ between the window and the slice;
# the two sharpest are `\bfoo` on "xfoo" at 1 (NO match --
# a context-blind engine matches) and `\bfoo` on
# "xfoo foo" at 1, which is (5,8) and not (1,4): a blind
# engine does not merely over-match, it reports the WRONG
# OCCURRENCE.
# ====================================================================

pattern \bfoo
features assertions
n ""
n "a"
n "x"
n " "
n "_"
n "1"
m "foo" 0 3
n "xfoo"
m " foo" 1 4
m "foo " 0 3
m "foox" 0 3
n "xfoofoo"
m "xfoo foo" 5 8
n "a x"
n "ab"
n "a b"
n "_a"
n "a_"
n "1a"
n "a1"
n "aa bb cc"
n "ab_cd ef"
n ".a."
n "a.b"
m "foobar" 0 3
n "a-b"
n "a\nb"
m "foo\n" 0 3
ns 0 "xfoo"
ns 1 "xfoo"
ns 2 "xfoo"
ns 3 "xfoo"
ns 4 "xfoo"
ms 0 " foo" 1 4
ms 1 " foo" 1 4
ns 2 " foo"
ns 3 " foo"
ns 4 " foo"
ms 0 "xfoo foo" 5 8
ms 1 "xfoo foo" 5 8
ms 2 "xfoo foo" 5 8
ms 3 "xfoo foo" 5 8
ms 4 "xfoo foo" 5 8
ms 5 "xfoo foo" 5 8
ns 6 "xfoo foo"
